// int32 arithmetic must trap on overflow, not wrap.
//
// The VM gained a fast path that computes signed 32 and 64 bit integers in native
// precision instead of the 128 bit form used for every width. That path is a duplicate of
// the arithmetic in the generic one, and a duplicate is only trustworthy if something
// checks that both agree at the boundaries. The existing width tests covered int8 and
// int128 -- both of which take the *generic* path -- so the fast path had no coverage at
// all before this file.
//
// Each case below is one the two implementations could disagree about: a sum that leaves
// the type but not the next width up, a product that leaves both, and a negative operand
// on the edge of the range.

function main():void {
    print("before\n");

    // max + 1: leaves int32, easy to miss if the check is done in 64 bits without a
    // bound test afterwards.
    int32 maxv = 2147483647;
    int32 one = 1;
    int32 boom = maxv + one;
    print(boom);
    eof: pass;
}
